WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:06.340
♪ The Simpsons ♪

2
00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:09.009
Children. you've been extra good.

3
00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:11.929
so you've earned
a very special field trip.

4
00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:15.933
Get ready for the best day
of your lives at the Waterpark…

5
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.935
[students cheering]

6
00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:22.856
-…Museum of Late Victorian Textiles.
-[students groaning]

7
00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.108
[groans]

8
00:00:25.192 --> 00:00:28.445
I saw the word "waterpark"
and got too excited to read the rest.

9
00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:30.614
[grunts]

10
00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:35.369
The entrance hall carpet
is a reproduction of an original

11
00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:39.039
that Mr. Waterpark saw
on a trip to Utrecht.

12
00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:41.208
Any guesses as to its ornamentation?

13
00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:42.709
[grunts]

14
00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:43.794
Anyone?

15
00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.712
Wilton. It's a Wilton pattern.

16
00:00:45.796 --> 00:00:49.132
You're the first person
that's ever gotten that.

17
00:00:49.216 --> 00:00:52.636
And a docent never forgets her first.

18
00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.055
[chuckles]

19
00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:56.765
[students] Ew.

20
00:00:56.848 --> 00:00:59.184
Kids. you've seen the foyer.

21
00:00:59.268 --> 00:01:02.980
Now let's visit the demi-foyer.

22
00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.815
[students groan]

23
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.652
Well. there's only one cure
for this type of boredom.

24
00:01:09.152 --> 00:01:12.030
A Gilded Age home invasion.

25
00:01:12.531 --> 00:01:13.574
[laughs]

26
00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:15.742
Nope. Nope.

27
00:01:15.826 --> 00:01:16.827
Nope.

28
00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.330
Ugh. There's gotta be something fun
in this dust dungeon.

29
00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.082
Hmm?

30
00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.461
-Whoa.
-[♪ dramatic music plays]

31
00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:30.799
Something.

32
00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:36.346
Don't be afraid to feel the perforations
on this Edwardian sable felt.

33
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.265
I'm not afraid.

34
00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:41.602
Psst.

35
00:01:41.685 --> 00:01:43.895
[♪ dramatic music plays]

36
00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:46.857
[all] Something.

37
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:52.195
[students giggling. cheering]

38
00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:56.908
I'm making it snow
over the lighthouse in St. Augustine.

39
00:02:00.746 --> 00:02:02.706
Snow globe fight!

40
00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:06.209
[students laughing. screaming]

41
00:02:06.293 --> 00:02:07.794
Hmm. this brocade.

42
00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:10.839
I've never felt such a luxurious warp.

43
00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:11.840
Twiddle the weft.

44
00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:14.051
-[glass shattering]
-[Bart] Eat globe!

45
00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.469
[gasps] My charges.

46
00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:18.096
[students clamoring]

47
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.514
[yelps]

48
00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:24.227
[groaning]

49
00:02:24.311 --> 00:02:26.480
This horseplay stops now.

50
00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:28.065
Globes down.

51
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.816
-Heads-up.
-Raise my head?

52
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.401
Heard and understood.

53
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:34.237
[glass shatters]

54
00:02:36.615 --> 00:02:39.159
[Skinner]
Oh. these past weeks have been difficult.

55
00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:42.037
Five eyeball surgeries. Two lid grafts.

56
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.498
Now I have rods. but no cones.

57
00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:45.957
But I have a purpose.

58
00:02:46.458 --> 00:02:50.754
Even though it's the last day
before spring break. I had to come back

59
00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:54.883
so I could say the words
every principal dreams of saying.

60
00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:56.968
You are garbage parents.

61
00:02:57.052 --> 00:02:58.553
Garbage parents?

62
00:02:58.637 --> 00:02:59.888
Even me?

63
00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:03.475
Yes. both of you spawned
that snow-globe-hurling monster.

64
00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:05.143
[both groan]

65
00:03:05.227 --> 00:03:06.978
He said. "Heads-up."

66
00:03:07.062 --> 00:03:08.980
And I raised my head accordingly.

67
00:03:10.941 --> 00:03:12.025
[both gasp]

68
00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:14.236
Please. Principal Skinner.

69
00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.030
Bart's not a bad kid.

70
00:03:17.114 --> 00:03:20.784
If only you could see
my special little guy the way I do.

71
00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.328
Come on. Marge.
Bart's the devil. and you know it.

72
00:03:23.412 --> 00:03:26.373
Anyway. it's your fault for taking
an amped-up psycho like that

73
00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:27.541
to a water park.

74
00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:29.668
It wasn't a water park.

75
00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:33.880
Oh. I didn't read past the first word
on the permission slip.

76
00:03:33.964 --> 00:03:35.507
All you parents are the same.

77
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.760
Take credit when your kids succeed
and make excuses when they fail.

78
00:03:38.844 --> 00:03:39.970
It's disgusting.

79
00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.471
It's because of parents like you that…

80
00:03:41.555 --> 00:03:44.516
[over PA] …I hate children!

81
00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:48.019
[sobs]

82
00:03:48.103 --> 00:03:51.106
[clears throat]
Disregard. Have a nice spring break.

83
00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:54.109
Mother. I'm home.

84
00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:56.820
[Agnes] Dear Seymour.

85
00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:00.240
I'm sorry I didn't visit you
in the hospital.

86
00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:03.535
Oh. No mother wants to see her son…

87
00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:05.036
period.

88
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.289
I'm going to Atlantic City with Jasper.

89
00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:12.210
It'll be a month of nonstop poker.
and we might get some card playing in too.

90
00:04:12.294 --> 00:04:13.545
[chuckles] Wink. wink.

91
00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:14.546
Ha!

92
00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:16.798
Must be nice to be able to wink. [sighs]

93
00:04:16.882 --> 00:04:17.883
Just as well.

94
00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:20.802
I have a whole spring break
to do what I wanna do.

95
00:04:20.886 --> 00:04:22.304
[crickets chirping]

96
00:04:22.387 --> 00:04:26.975
Ah. Textbook requisition forms
and room temperature Mr. Pibb.

97
00:04:27.058 --> 00:04:28.727
This is the high life.

98
00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.187
-[loud clattering]
-[stammers]

99
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.271
Who goes there?

100
00:04:31.354 --> 00:04:32.355
[clattering continues]

101
00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:33.356
[skittering]

102
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.025
That's no who.

103
00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:36.359
It's a what.

104
00:04:38.653 --> 00:04:42.073
[Willie]
Go away. I'm about to score with my wife.

105
00:04:42.157 --> 00:04:44.117
But there's a creature loose
in the school.

106
00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.203
Why didn't you say so. man?

107
00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:49.956
Our Scottish Scrabble game can wait.

108
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.458
[♪ bagpipe music plays]

109
00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:53.960
Now go kill a frightened.
defenseless animal

110
00:04:54.044 --> 00:04:56.421
that stumbled into the world of man.

111
00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:01.676
[♪ dramatic music plays]

112
00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:05.764
[thudding]

113
00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.021
The creature.

114
00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:16.274
[♪ dramatic music continues]

115
00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:28.995
I'm a ghost of a kid who died long ago

116
00:05:29.079 --> 00:05:33.250
when kids used to die all the time.

117
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:34.626
A specter!

118
00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.671
Those weren't dog bones
I found in the sandbox!

119
00:05:37.754 --> 00:05:41.007
You're no apparition.
You've been living in my library.

120
00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:43.009
Living in a library?

121
00:05:43.093 --> 00:05:46.513
[scoffs] That's sick. man.
How can you say that to a little kid?

122
00:05:46.596 --> 00:05:48.431
Now get off my lawn.

123
00:05:48.515 --> 00:05:50.934
A student holing up in the school.

124
00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:52.936
How can I live this down?

125
00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:56.731
A blemish like this
on my permanent record.

126
00:05:56.815 --> 00:06:00.193
[laughing] What the hell is coming out
of your eyes. you freak?

127
00:06:00.277 --> 00:06:01.987
It's whimsical snow.

128
00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:07.993
I recognize you. Your name is Hub.

129
00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.119
You're a student here.

130
00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:12.998
This blatant infraction
is going directly into your file.

131
00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:16.251
Well. good luck with that.
sparkle tears. I'm not in there.

132
00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:18.461
You're not enrolled? But I've seen you.

133
00:06:18.545 --> 00:06:20.171
You're in Ms. Peyton's class.

134
00:06:20.255 --> 00:06:21.631
One of those kids that sits in the back

135
00:06:21.715 --> 00:06:25.176
between Eyeglasses Girl
and Bucktooth Boy and never speaks.

136
00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.888
Good Lord. You've been pretending
to be a student

137
00:06:27.971 --> 00:06:29.598
so you could live at the school.

138
00:06:29.681 --> 00:06:31.558
[mocking] So you could live at the school.

139
00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:33.059
Live at the school.

140
00:06:33.143 --> 00:06:37.439
[garbled mocking] …school.

141
00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:38.690
Where are your parents?

142
00:06:38.773 --> 00:06:39.941
Where are your parents?

143
00:06:40.025 --> 00:06:41.860
My mother's with Jasper.

144
00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:45.447
and my father's ashes are in a bag
in the upstairs sewing room.

145
00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:46.531
Now come clean.

146
00:06:46.615 --> 00:06:48.825
Seymour. what the devil is going on?

147
00:06:48.909 --> 00:06:50.243
Look out! It's your pimp.

148
00:06:50.327 --> 00:06:51.745
He's not my pimp.

149
00:06:51.828 --> 00:06:54.539
I've got this. sir.
I'm calling Child Services.

150
00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:56.708
You do. and I will cut you like a bitch.

151
00:06:56.791 --> 00:06:57.876
[gasps]

152
00:06:57.959 --> 00:07:01.963
If the state learns a runaway child
has been squatting here.

153
00:07:02.047 --> 00:07:06.092
they'll turn this school
into a Spirit Halloween store. okay?

154
00:07:06.176 --> 00:07:08.428
You keep this child under your supervision

155
00:07:08.511 --> 00:07:11.348
until you locate his family
and return him to them.

156
00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.183
I'm going back to bed.

157
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.978
The next time I see you.
you better have my money…

158
00:07:17.062 --> 00:07:20.690
requisition forms
for next year's textbooks.

159
00:07:21.358 --> 00:07:23.568
You can tell he really values you.

160
00:07:23.652 --> 00:07:27.072
Don't get comfortable.
You're only here till I find your parents.

161
00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:31.159
Look. Principal Skinner. you're not going
to find them. They're dead.

162
00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:33.662
Oh. my Lord. I'm so sorry.

163
00:07:33.745 --> 00:07:34.746
It was horrible.

164
00:07:34.829 --> 00:07:37.957
Their hot-air balloon drifted over
North Korea on Kim Jong-Un's birthday

165
00:07:38.041 --> 00:07:40.293
and was hit by a celebratory ICBM.

166
00:07:40.377 --> 00:07:42.587
-[muffled laughter]
-Outright fibbery!

167
00:07:42.671 --> 00:07:45.632
Oh. my God. You are so dumb.

168
00:07:45.715 --> 00:07:47.842
How did you live this long?

169
00:07:47.926 --> 00:07:50.387
You don't give me respect.
then you don't get any.

170
00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.306
You are not leaving
Mother's sewing room until…

171
00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:55.934
[gasps. grunts]

172
00:07:56.518 --> 00:07:58.269
Give me strength. Papa.

173
00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.939
Get back in here.

174
00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:03.733
Aw. But I was gonna go out
and find America.

175
00:08:05.068 --> 00:08:06.277
[groans]

176
00:08:06.361 --> 00:08:09.030
This was fun. but I gotta go.
You've been a great host.

177
00:08:09.114 --> 00:08:10.615
Five stars.

178
00:08:11.282 --> 00:08:12.283
[sighs]

179
00:08:12.367 --> 00:08:15.704
It's time to swallow
my pride and ask for help.

180
00:08:15.787 --> 00:08:16.788
[doorbell rings]

181
00:08:17.539 --> 00:08:20.333
Marge. a man is here.

182
00:08:20.417 --> 00:08:21.459
Principal Skinner?

183
00:08:21.543 --> 00:08:23.712
Did you come to yell at us again?

184
00:08:23.795 --> 00:08:25.505
Mr. and Mrs. Simpson.

185
00:08:26.214 --> 00:08:27.966
I come to you hat in hand.

186
00:08:28.633 --> 00:08:30.844
I've never had to deal with a child

187
00:08:30.927 --> 00:08:34.139
outside the hours of 8:10 to 2:45.

188
00:08:34.723 --> 00:08:38.560
I need your advice.
How do I handle this handful?

189
00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:41.396
[both laughing]

190
00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:44.023
You're asking us? The garbage parents?

191
00:08:44.107 --> 00:08:47.152
[cackles] But we spawned a monster.

192
00:08:47.235 --> 00:08:50.572
[Simpsons cackling]

193
00:08:50.655 --> 00:08:53.324
One day with a kid.
and he's already broken.

194
00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.412
And now he needs our help.

195
00:09:01.833 --> 00:09:03.585
Please. I'm desperate.

196
00:09:04.169 --> 00:09:07.255
Oh. my God. Oh. that's so sweet.

197
00:09:07.338 --> 00:09:10.425
Okay. okay. [chuckles]
Where's the kid now?

198
00:09:10.508 --> 00:09:13.094
In my house. I hired Nelson to sit.

199
00:09:13.178 --> 00:09:15.889
I'm cold. Can you turn the heat up?

200
00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:16.973
You're the client.

201
00:09:17.057 --> 00:09:18.183
[farts]

202
00:09:19.184 --> 00:09:22.854
Please. I'm at my wits' end.
I need your help with Hub.

203
00:09:22.937 --> 00:09:25.065
Okay. because you begged nicely.

204
00:09:25.148 --> 00:09:28.777
we'll share a couple
of nuggets of parental wisdom.

205
00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.489
Sometimes. challenging kids
just wanna be recognized.

206
00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:36.493
Find what the little miracle
is interested in and encourage it.

207
00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:40.371
Be a hard-ass. Every day should be
like his first day in prison.

208
00:09:40.455 --> 00:09:43.208
and he's all alone
in the yard without a shiv.

209
00:09:43.291 --> 00:09:44.709
Flood him with hugs.

210
00:09:44.793 --> 00:09:45.960
Crush his spirit.

211
00:09:46.044 --> 00:09:47.879
Give him a shoulder to cry on.

212
00:09:47.962 --> 00:09:49.297
Quit your crying!

213
00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.007
[Marge] Tell him he's loved.

214
00:09:51.091 --> 00:09:54.219
-[Homer] Tell him he's lucky to be alive.
-[Marge] Nourish his creativity.

215
00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:56.096
[Homer] Lock him in a dog crate.

216
00:09:58.014 --> 00:10:01.226
Good Lord.
Someone could slip on this watery plastic.

217
00:10:01.309 --> 00:10:02.977
Or worse. slide.

218
00:10:03.061 --> 00:10:04.646
Wet orphan coming through.

219
00:10:04.729 --> 00:10:06.272
[both] Whoa. whoa!

220
00:10:06.356 --> 00:10:08.441
Whoa. whoa!

221
00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:14.030
Oh. Uh-oh. We meet again. Darth Tedious.

222
00:10:14.114 --> 00:10:15.490
[laughs]

223
00:10:15.573 --> 00:10:20.328
Give this kid a break.
His parents died on the Titanic.

224
00:10:21.496 --> 00:10:24.582
[♪ Muzak playing over speakers]

225
00:10:24.666 --> 00:10:26.501
Good afternoon. Seymour.

226
00:10:26.584 --> 00:10:28.253
Here for your usual?

227
00:10:30.255 --> 00:10:31.965
Oh. no. thank you. Phillip.

228
00:10:32.048 --> 00:10:35.593
We need to get this ragamuffin
some respectable schoolboy clothes.

229
00:10:35.677 --> 00:10:41.766
Hmm. Perhaps a poplin pullover
and corduroy perma-crease slacks.

230
00:10:45.436 --> 00:10:46.688
[groans]

231
00:10:47.897 --> 00:10:52.360
[Hub] ♪ The world's most boring loser
Is making me dress just like him ♪

232
00:10:52.443 --> 00:10:54.696
♪ His house smells like old lady pee ♪

233
00:10:54.779 --> 00:11:00.243
♪ But he's so used to the smell
That he can't smell it ♪

234
00:11:00.326 --> 00:11:01.536
Will you get on with it?

235
00:11:02.954 --> 00:11:05.707
By Doug Henning's mustache. He's escaped.

236
00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.167
[screams. grunts]

237
00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:08.626
[panting]

238
00:11:10.587 --> 00:11:11.588
Hub!

239
00:11:18.303 --> 00:11:20.221
-There you are.
-[gasps]

240
00:11:21.389 --> 00:11:23.516
-Come out of there right now.
-Make me.

241
00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:28.396
[gasping]
My greasy fingers won't let me… [grunts]

242
00:11:28.479 --> 00:11:30.982
There's no lifeguard. Hang on. boy.

243
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:38.074
Watch Online Movies and Series for FREE
www.osdb.link/lm

244
00:11:44.412 --> 00:11:45.455
[coughs]

245
00:11:45.538 --> 00:11:46.915
[gasping]

246
00:11:48.499 --> 00:11:50.960
[coughs] You saved me.

247
00:11:51.044 --> 00:11:54.255
All I've done is
say mean stuff to you. run away

248
00:11:54.339 --> 00:11:57.800
and scratch up all your records
where dogs bark Christmas carols.

249
00:11:57.884 --> 00:12:00.470
It's fine.
Those dogs are long dead anyway.

250
00:12:00.553 --> 00:12:02.805
It's not like I can
go see them in concert again.

251
00:12:02.889 --> 00:12:05.308
But of course I saved you.
You were in danger.

252
00:12:05.391 --> 00:12:06.517
Thanks. Seymour.

253
00:12:07.018 --> 00:12:10.605
In all my years as a principal.
I've… I've never been hugged.

254
00:12:10.688 --> 00:12:13.191
I feel like I'm floating on air.

255
00:12:13.274 --> 00:12:15.276
[table fan blowing]

256
00:12:18.029 --> 00:12:22.242
[♪ barking "Jingle Bells"]

257
00:12:22.325 --> 00:12:24.786
Well. that's marvelous.
You're very talented.

258
00:12:24.869 --> 00:12:26.371
Look out. Perry Como.

259
00:12:26.454 --> 00:12:29.040
Oh. I like how you know people
I've never heard of.

260
00:12:29.123 --> 00:12:32.043
Ah. spoken like a young Mort Sahl.

261
00:12:32.126 --> 00:12:35.630
[♪ "Room 222 - Theme" playing]

262
00:12:50.812 --> 00:12:55.400
Well. so ends the first spring break
where I actually had fun.

263
00:12:55.483 --> 00:12:58.736
[sighs] Well. I suppose I must
resume the hunt for your parents.

264
00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:00.738
Oh. well. good luck with that.

265
00:13:01.239 --> 00:13:02.782
They were on a Japanese game show

266
00:13:02.865 --> 00:13:05.493
and they drowned in a giant toilet
full of salmon roe.

267
00:13:05.576 --> 00:13:06.828
[chuckles]

268
00:13:06.911 --> 00:13:09.664
You and your fantastical falsehoods.

269
00:13:09.747 --> 00:13:13.626
[sighs] Well. catch you later.
Time to lone-wolf it again.

270
00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:16.004
Returning to the background.

271
00:13:16.087 --> 00:13:18.089
[students chattering]

272
00:13:20.758 --> 00:13:22.927
Nourish his creativity.

273
00:13:24.178 --> 00:13:26.139
Nope. No talent. Tone deaf.

274
00:13:26.222 --> 00:13:29.892
Dance. ten. Looks. three.
Ooh. Principal Skinner.

275
00:13:29.976 --> 00:13:33.062
Dewey. you've got one more auditionee.

276
00:13:33.146 --> 00:13:35.940
I don't wanna be
in some stupid bunny musical.

277
00:13:36.024 --> 00:13:39.068
I thought we were cool.
I went to see Laser Beethoven with you.

278
00:13:39.152 --> 00:13:42.864
The weird smoke there made me fall asleep.
Don't make me do this.

279
00:13:43.573 --> 00:13:45.658
Be a hard-ass. Make him do it.

280
00:13:45.742 --> 00:13:48.244
If you don't. I will.

281
00:13:48.995 --> 00:13:50.580
I'm not giving you the choice.

282
00:13:50.663 --> 00:13:52.832
You're a talented kid
with untapped potential.

283
00:13:52.915 --> 00:13:54.292
Now get up there and audition.

284
00:13:57.253 --> 00:13:59.047
Why did I make Hub audition?

285
00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:02.258
He's probably dying
of embarrassment in there.

286
00:14:02.341 --> 00:14:04.010
Seymour. you're a fool.

287
00:14:04.093 --> 00:14:08.389
You have a master's degree in education.
but the parenting skills of a basketball.

288
00:14:10.725 --> 00:14:14.604
-I'm sorry. Hub.
-Okay. I tried out like you said.

289
00:14:14.687 --> 00:14:15.688
They hated me.

290
00:14:16.689 --> 00:14:18.399
For being so awesome.

291
00:14:18.483 --> 00:14:21.152
I'm playing Farmer McGregor.
I'm in three scenes.

292
00:14:21.235 --> 00:14:22.361
One with Peter Rabbit.

293
00:14:22.445 --> 00:14:24.363
[gasps] Aw!

294
00:14:24.447 --> 00:14:27.366
I did it. I'm parenting.
and I'm good at it.

295
00:14:28.076 --> 00:14:31.913
How do you like me now.
Spalding Dirk Nowitzki autograph special?

296
00:14:36.042 --> 00:14:39.879
Okay. daffodils. daffodils.
Spring has sprung.

297
00:14:39.962 --> 00:14:41.297
Peter Rabbit comes out.

298
00:14:41.881 --> 00:14:44.050
And cue Farmer McGregor.

299
00:14:44.133 --> 00:14:46.677
[in Southern accent]
Who's been eating my carrots?

300
00:14:46.761 --> 00:14:48.179
Exit McGregor.

301
00:14:48.262 --> 00:14:51.974
Peter Rabbit launches into his big number.
"I've Been Eating the Carrots."

302
00:14:52.058 --> 00:14:54.143
That's it? That's all you got for Hub?

303
00:14:54.227 --> 00:14:55.228
It's not all.

304
00:14:55.311 --> 00:14:57.688
He sweeps up the bandannas
after the hoedown scene.

305
00:14:57.772 --> 00:15:00.358
Dewey. I'm starting
to think you lack the vision

306
00:15:00.441 --> 00:15:02.068
to make these characters come to life.

307
00:15:02.151 --> 00:15:05.655
And you could do better?
Fine. Here's the headset.

308
00:15:05.738 --> 00:15:08.533
You want it? Take it. take it.
Just-- Just take it.

309
00:15:08.616 --> 00:15:10.660
[muttering angrily]

310
00:15:10.743 --> 00:15:14.580
Well. cast. um.
who's ready to go on a ride with me

311
00:15:14.664 --> 00:15:17.834
as we explore the depths
of our new protagonist.

312
00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:19.585
-Farmer McGregor?
-[♪ piano riff plays]

313
00:15:19.669 --> 00:15:23.214
Okay. so. the thing you guys don't get
about Farmer McGregor

314
00:15:23.297 --> 00:15:27.426
is he's the best actor in the world.
and you're just a bunch of dumb animals.

315
00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:28.761
Now. let's have fun with this.

316
00:15:28.845 --> 00:15:30.805
What the hell is this [bleep]?

317
00:15:30.888 --> 00:15:32.974
Farmer McGregor only has three lines

318
00:15:33.057 --> 00:15:36.644
and two of them are.
"Here comes Peter Rabbit." Me.

319
00:15:36.727 --> 00:15:37.728
We're gonna fix that.

320
00:15:37.812 --> 00:15:41.983
Lisa Simpson.
I need a rewrite by third period tomorrow.

321
00:15:42.066 --> 00:15:45.528
Beef up the McGregor character.
Explore his inner world.

322
00:15:45.611 --> 00:15:47.780
Give Hub something worthy of his talents.

323
00:15:47.864 --> 00:15:49.282
Fix the script.

324
00:15:49.365 --> 00:15:52.034
I get to be
every eight-year-old girl's hero:

325
00:15:52.118 --> 00:15:53.953
Steven Zaillian.

326
00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:58.791
Milhouse is a joy to have in the room.

327
00:15:58.875 --> 00:16:03.880
He just needs to stop practicing
his kissing on female lunch box images.

328
00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:06.007
You hear that. Luann? Female.

329
00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:12.221
These thin-skinned parents unable
to handle the slightest feedback

330
00:16:12.305 --> 00:16:15.308
about their perfect children.
[sighs] Who's next?

331
00:16:15.391 --> 00:16:19.812
That would be Hub. but his parents
didn't sign up for the conference. so…

332
00:16:19.896 --> 00:16:21.230
I'll stand in for them.

333
00:16:21.314 --> 00:16:24.984
Okay. well.
I-I can get to Hub's strengths in a sec.

334
00:16:25.067 --> 00:16:27.570
but I'll start with some of his stretches.

335
00:16:27.653 --> 00:16:29.655
[scoffs] Well. that won't take long.

336
00:16:29.739 --> 00:16:34.118
Yes. he used to be
so quiet and unassuming.

337
00:16:34.202 --> 00:16:39.457
but now that he's starring in the play.
he's become a bit of a show-off.

338
00:16:40.416 --> 00:16:42.251
You're fired.

339
00:16:42.335 --> 00:16:45.296
But I… No. no.
I-I'm merely pointing out that Hub--

340
00:16:45.379 --> 00:16:47.506
Is coming into his own.

341
00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:49.634
He's talented. He's a supernova.

342
00:16:49.717 --> 00:16:52.720
What are you? A fourth-grade teacher
in a failing school?

343
00:16:52.803 --> 00:16:54.931
And this boy is an orphan.

344
00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:59.352
His parents both had Benjamin Button
disease and died as 85-year-old babies.

345
00:16:59.435 --> 00:17:00.770
Hmm. That's another thing.

346
00:17:00.853 --> 00:17:02.980
Hub has a tendency to…

347
00:17:04.023 --> 00:17:05.024
lie.

348
00:17:05.107 --> 00:17:06.275
He's an actor.

349
00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:08.486
Actors lie for a living. That's their art.

350
00:17:08.569 --> 00:17:11.113
Don't you dare say
another harsh word about my…

351
00:17:11.822 --> 00:17:15.034
-Hub.
-Seymour. you are his principal.

352
00:17:15.117 --> 00:17:16.869
not his father.

353
00:17:16.953 --> 00:17:19.705
[sighs] Of course. You're right.

354
00:17:19.789 --> 00:17:22.041
You've given me a lot to think about.

355
00:17:27.421 --> 00:17:31.133
Excellent. Willie. Forty down. 360 to go.

356
00:17:31.217 --> 00:17:34.470
Ooh. Look who's coming to town. Hub.

357
00:17:40.893 --> 00:17:42.645
Well. this is your big night.

358
00:17:42.728 --> 00:17:44.730
They're all here to see you.

359
00:17:44.814 --> 00:17:47.275
And I'm ready to bring the thunder.

360
00:17:47.358 --> 00:17:49.652
Look out. theatergoers. here comes…

361
00:17:50.695 --> 00:17:53.030
Holy crud.

362
00:17:53.114 --> 00:17:54.156
Butterflies. eh?

363
00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:56.867
From Brando to Balki.
all the great ones get 'em.

364
00:17:58.077 --> 00:18:00.580
Break a leg. son.

365
00:18:06.002 --> 00:18:09.505
[♪ dramatic music plays]

366
00:18:10.756 --> 00:18:13.467
Peter Rabbit is dead.

367
00:18:13.551 --> 00:18:15.553
Who will save the farm?

368
00:18:15.636 --> 00:18:19.015
-[♪ drum roll]
-[all chanting] McGregor. McGregor.

369
00:18:20.016 --> 00:18:22.435
-[♪ music fizzles out]
-[audience murmuring]

370
00:18:22.518 --> 00:18:25.187
That's your cue. Olivier.

371
00:18:26.522 --> 00:18:27.523
Hub!

372
00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:30.526
The adoption.

373
00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:31.986
I spooked him.

374
00:18:32.069 --> 00:18:33.863
Hub. wait. Come back.

375
00:18:35.031 --> 00:18:36.032
Dad!

376
00:18:36.699 --> 00:18:39.827
He called me Dad.
He wants me to adopt him.

377
00:18:40.328 --> 00:18:42.788
Son. you just made me the happiest…

378
00:18:42.872 --> 00:18:44.123
What are you doing here?

379
00:18:44.206 --> 00:18:46.083
We're your parents. That's what.

380
00:18:46.667 --> 00:18:50.046
Ha-ha! You thought he was running to you.

381
00:18:50.129 --> 00:18:51.672
Your mother dances for coupons.

382
00:18:51.756 --> 00:18:54.759
and your father has a second family
he loves more than you.

383
00:18:54.842 --> 00:18:56.510
[whimpers]

384
00:18:56.594 --> 00:18:57.803
How did you find me?

385
00:18:57.887 --> 00:19:02.016
Your face is on every light pole
from here to Ogdenville.

386
00:19:02.099 --> 00:19:04.560
[sighs] I… I don't understand.

387
00:19:04.644 --> 00:19:05.645
We don't either.

388
00:19:05.728 --> 00:19:08.856
You've got some explaining to do.
Alexander Hubley.

389
00:19:08.939 --> 00:19:10.024
[groans]

390
00:19:10.107 --> 00:19:12.026
Mom and Dad dumped me at boarding school

391
00:19:12.109 --> 00:19:15.321
with a bunch of other snooty jerks
with rich parents who don't give a crap.

392
00:19:15.404 --> 00:19:19.325
I couldn't take it. so I bailed
and came to Springfield. I liked it here.

393
00:19:19.408 --> 00:19:22.203
And now we've found you.

394
00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:24.330
Yeah. well.
it only took you like five months.

395
00:19:24.413 --> 00:19:27.583
We're sorry. son. Come on. Let's go home.

396
00:19:30.378 --> 00:19:32.129
Uh. ladies and gentlemen. uh.

397
00:19:32.213 --> 00:19:35.966
due to an administrator getting
his heart broken into a million pieces.

398
00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:38.010
we are canceling the play.

399
00:19:38.094 --> 00:19:40.346
[audience cheering]

400
00:19:40.429 --> 00:19:42.515
Yeah.
I hear your cheers of disappointment.

401
00:19:42.598 --> 00:19:45.685
but. unfortunately.
we have lost our Farmer McGregor.

402
00:19:45.768 --> 00:19:47.561
No. you haven't.

403
00:19:47.645 --> 00:19:49.522
[audience groaning]

404
00:19:50.856 --> 00:19:52.858
A farmer rises with the sun.

405
00:19:52.942 --> 00:19:54.652
coaxing life out of the earth

406
00:19:54.735 --> 00:19:57.488
and nourishing the beasts
under his dominion.

407
00:19:57.988 --> 00:20:00.991
But I wanna talk about
one particular creature on my farm.

408
00:20:01.075 --> 00:20:04.704
A wayward mouse who had been
abandoned by his rich mouse parents

409
00:20:04.787 --> 00:20:06.163
at an elite boarding farm.

410
00:20:06.247 --> 00:20:09.417
Wait. I didn't write
any of this mouse junk.

411
00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:12.503
You didn't write anything.
You just ran the room.

412
00:20:12.586 --> 00:20:15.756
Picking good stuff is just
as hard as creating good stuff.

413
00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:19.844
The mouse ran away and hid in the barn.
blending in with the other animals.

414
00:20:19.927 --> 00:20:22.263
afraid to call attention to himself.

415
00:20:22.346 --> 00:20:24.140
Then. a kind old mouse found him.

416
00:20:24.223 --> 00:20:26.600
He shared with him
the cheese of encouragement.

417
00:20:26.684 --> 00:20:29.603
And with that. the mouse spread his wings

418
00:20:29.687 --> 00:20:33.357
and became a magnificent
Mr. Pibb-drinking butterfly.

419
00:20:33.441 --> 00:20:34.942
[cooing]

420
00:20:35.025 --> 00:20:37.445
But now. the little mouse has to leave.

421
00:20:37.528 --> 00:20:41.157
and he'll never forget the kind
older mouse who believed in him.

422
00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:44.285
allowing him to see more.

423
00:20:45.494 --> 00:20:47.788
[audience exclaiming and applauding]

424
00:20:51.542 --> 00:20:54.420
Your son is terrific.

425
00:20:54.503 --> 00:20:55.504
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

426
00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:58.674
That's not my boy.

427
00:21:08.184 --> 00:21:10.186
[♪ theme music playing]

428
00:21:16.442 --> 00:21:18.360
Okay. guys. let's focus.

429
00:21:18.444 --> 00:21:21.989
Again. how does McGregor lose the farm
at the end of the first act?

430
00:21:22.573 --> 00:21:24.909
Anyone? Anything!

431
00:21:24.992 --> 00:21:26.494
How about this?

432
00:21:26.577 --> 00:21:30.664
McGregor comes home. house gone.
rabbits everywhere.

433
00:21:30.748 --> 00:21:32.792
and he says…

434
00:21:32.875 --> 00:21:35.461
Don't start a sentence
if you don't know where it's going!

435
00:21:35.544 --> 00:21:39.423
[groans] Okay. guess what.
Now we're staying late.

436
00:21:39.507 --> 00:21:41.175
What do you guys want for dinner?

437
00:21:41.258 --> 00:21:43.177
-Corn dogs!
-Dino nuggets!

438
00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.845
-Noodles with butter!
-Tuna fish!

439
00:21:44.929 --> 00:21:48.057
-Pepperoni!
-Now you've got pitches?

439
00:21:49.305 --> 00:22:49.425
